FROM: Andrew LaMarche <andrewjlamarche@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] octeon: keep PCIe BAR allocations below 4GB

The root-complex memory windows registered by octeon_pcie_setup()
span 64 bits of bus address (mem_resource covers 4GB-256MB up to
mem_base + 2^36), so the PCI core places 64-bit BARs above 4GB. On
a CN7030 board with two QCA9984 wifi chips each BAR0 landed at bus
address 0x100000000 -- low half 0x00000000, high half 0x1.

That placement is fatal for endpoints whose inbound memory window is
32-bit internally even though their PCIe config space advertises a
64-bit BAR. The QCA9984 routes inbound memory requests through a
32-bit CORE_CTRL/BAR0-start register (see the "target writes BAR to
CORE_CTRL" comment in the ath10k driver), and after the chip's
SOC_GLOBAL_RESET -- which ath10k issues unconditionally during
probe -- its boot ROM re-establishes that window from the low half
of the BAR only. The chip then stops decoding the address the host
maps it at, while config space keeps working: setpci shows the full
BAR and command register intact, but every MMIO read is a failed
completion (devmem of the hardwired chip ID at BAR0+0xec returns
0xffffffff) and ath10k times out waiting for the firmware indicator
forever:

  ath10k_pci 0000:01:00.0: failed to receive initialized event from target: 00000000
  ath10k_pci 0000:01:00.0: failed to wait for target after cold reset: -145

The vendor SDK kernel avoids this by keeping the bootloader's 32-bit
BAR assignment (0xe0000000). Cap the controller memory windows at
the end of 32-bit bus address space so every device BAR is placed
where 32-bit-only endpoints can decode it. The window start already
sits at 4GB-256MB -- the x86 "big BAR" convention this code
emulates -- so each port keeps 256MB of device address space, ample
for the wifi and switch cards these SoCs ship with, and matching
what x86 BIOSes do by default. Device DMA (inbound) is unaffected:
it is translated by the BAR2 window, not by these resources.

Signed-off-by: Andrew LaMarche <andrewjlamarche@gmail.com>

--- a/arch/mips/pci/pcie-octeon.c	2026-10-28 11:40:27
+++ b/arch/mips/pci/pcie-octeon.c	2026-10-28 11:40:27
@@ -2089,9 +2089,22 @@
 			octeon_pcie0_controller.mem_resource->start =
 				cvmx_pcie_get_mem_base_address(0) +
 				(4ul << 30) - (OCTEON_PCI_BAR1_HOLE_SIZE << 20);
+			/*
+			 * Keep the window inside the first 4GB of bus
+			 * address space. Some endpoints with 64-bit
+			 * BARs (e.g. the QCA9984 wifi chip) decode
+			 * inbound memory through a 32-bit internal
+			 * window that their boot ROM restores from the
+			 * low half of the BAR after a chip reset; a BAR
+			 * placed above 4GB stops being decoded as soon
+			 * as the driver resets the chip, while config
+			 * space keeps working. Capping the end forces
+			 * 32-bit BAR placement, like the x86 BIOS
+			 * behavior this window already emulates.
+			 */
 			octeon_pcie0_controller.mem_resource->end =
 				cvmx_pcie_get_mem_base_address(0) +
-				cvmx_pcie_get_mem_size(0) - 1;
+				(4ull << 30) - 1;
 			/*
 			 * Ports must be above 16KB for the ISA bus
 			 * filtering in the PCI-X to PCI bridge.
@@ -2171,7 +2184,7 @@
 				(OCTEON_PCI_BAR1_HOLE_SIZE << 20);
 			octeon_pcie1_controller.mem_resource->end =
 				cvmx_pcie_get_mem_base_address(1) +
-				cvmx_pcie_get_mem_size(1) - 1;
+				(4ull << 30) - 1;
 			/*
 			 * Ports must be above 16KB for the ISA bus filtering
 			 * in the PCI-X to PCI bridge.
